This assignment has three parts: sketches, an infographic, and a reflection.

Create a digital infographic demonstrating the complexity of a keyword (or one of the terms we’ve connected to that keyword), drawing from and citing at least three sources.

Begin by researching data and sources relevant to a course keyword. You must draw from (and cite) at least three sources that will allow you to demonstrate the complexity of your keyword. 

After the research stage, you must sketch out your infographic in your journal at least twice, demonstrating the evolution of your design. Submit a photo of your sketches with your assignment. Assignments without accompanying sketches will receive zero.

Use your research and sketches to create an infographic. Your infographic must:

Make an argument. 
Clearly establish your term and its importance.
Use at least 3 substantial sources of information about your term.
Convey information clearly and concisely so that your poster is not overwhelmed by text
Use graphical elements wisely to communicate and reinforce your ideas; you can use handmade graphical elements (e.g. drawings, collages, etc) 
Create a pleasing aesthetic effect.
Convey resources (including information sources, sources for visual images, tools used, etc.) appropriately on your poster itself, and also provide full citation information in your reflection.
You may use any medium you like for your infographic. It can be a drawing, a collage, or a papier-maché, or created with a tool like Canva, easel.ly or piktochart, some of which allow for interactivity.
MAX 300 words in infographic (less is likely better).

Reflection: 

Write a short reflection on the process and outcomes of creating your infographic. It should address some of the following questions:

How does your infographic compare to those we studied in class? Did it convey the information the way you intended? Was anything missing? In what ways is it visually successful? Is it verbally successful? 
How did your infographic evolve from the sketches to the final product?
What did you learn about how infographics convey information? Do you see infographics differently now that you have represented data this way? Explain in your insights into the visual interpretation of data, how we interact with infographics, any problems with using infographics to represent information, or other observations relating to the keywords we’ve discussed in class so far.
Length: Max. 250 words
Include a Works Cited page with citations for the sources. 
